 ### [Single-kernel Octree Traversal](15600-single-kernel-octree-traversal.md)
-A technique for traversing an octree for ray tracing by performing the entire traversal logic (including recursion simulation) within a single CUDA kernel execution per ray, rather than using multiple kernel launches for each recursion depth [15600-single-kernel-octree-traversal.md].
-
-#### ESVO (Efficient Sparse Voxel Octrees)
-A port of the 'Efficient Sparse Voxel Octrees' algorithm, specifically excluding contour generation and beam optimization features [S7, unofficial]. The traversal is a near 1:1 port of the original ESVO implementation. Node encoding was changed: relative pointers are 'backwards' to allow trees to be built without intermediate buffers (parent nodes appear after children), and far pointers are signaled by a reserved upper range rather than a dedicated bit [S7, unofficial]. Memory cost is approximately 4 bytes per node. Nodes are very small (4 bytes each) and localized, with a z-curve layout resulting from building the tree in depth-first order [S7, unofficial]. Performance is underwhelming because it takes many extra iterations to ascend and descend the tree and advance positions by DDA [S7, unofficial]. Editing cost is approximately O(log m), leveraging path copying for persistent data structures [S7, unofficial]. On Sponza 2k, ESVO achieves 95.0 Mrays/s for primary rays and 66.2 Mrays/s for primary + 1 diffuse bounce [S7, unofficial].
-
-#### Non-Intersectable Node Omission
-Optimization to avoid adding children to the stack if they cannot possibly intersect the ray closer than the current best intersection t. It uses precomputed slab boundaries of children [S4, unofficial]. If a child's minimum slab boundary $t_{i1}$ (for $i \in \{x,y,z\}$) is less than the current best intersection $t_{min}$, the child is guaranteed not to intersect closer than $t_{min}$ and is skipped [S4, unofficial]. Similarly, if a child's maximum slab boundary $t_{i0}$ is greater than $t_{max}$, the child is skipped [S4, unofficial]. Factors separating children of a node are defined as $((tx_0, ty_0, tz_0) + (tx_1, ty_1, tz_1)) \times 0.5$ [S4, unofficial].
+Single-kernel octree traversal is a technique for traversing an octree for ray tracing by performing the entire traversal logic, including recursion simulation, within a single CUDA kernel execution per ray, rather than using multiple kernel launches for each recursion depth. It is preferred over multi-kernel recursion because kernel invocation overhead is high. Although single-kernel traversal may cause thread divergence due to the SIMD nature of GPUs, coherent rays in a warp traverse similarly, mitigating this issue.
+
+### [ESVO (Efficient Sparse Voxel Octrees)](3089-esvo-efficient-sparse-voxel-octrees.md)
+ESVO is a GPU-based ray casting algorithm that uses a Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) to reduce voxel storage costs and enable real-time rendering of large scenes. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by using a compact 4-byte node encoding with a depth-first (z-curve) memory layout, which optimizes for localized memory access patterns on the GPU. Unlike [Hierarchical Ray Marching](5238-hierarchical-ray-marching.md) which may use larger leaf nodes or different traversal strategies, ESVO relies on a near 1:1 port of the original implementation with specific pointer encoding changes to facilitate buffer-less tree construction.
+
+### Non-Intersectable Node Omission
+Optimization to avoid adding children to the stack if they cannot possibly intersect the ray closer than the current best intersection $t$ [S4, unofficial]. It uses precomputed slab boundaries of children [S4, unofficial]. If a child's minimum slab boundary $t_{i1}$ (for $i \in \{x,y,z\}$) is less than the current best intersection $t_{min}$, the child is guaranteed not to intersect closer than $t_{min}$ and is skipped [S4, unofficial]. Similarly, if a child's maximum slab boundary $t_{i0}$ is greater than $t_{max}$, the child is skipped [S4, unofficial]. Factors separating children of a node are defined as $((tx_0, ty_0, tz_0) + (tx_1, ty_1, tz_1)) \times 0.5$ [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** O(log s) in the ideal case, where s is the initial size of the Octree. The leaf raycasting is O(1) as it always processes a fixed 32x32x32 volume [S2, unofficial]. In the worst case (e.g., diagonal traversal through dense regions), it can approach O(s · log s) [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** The stack simulation uses fixed-size arrays (e.g., depth 4), resulting in O(1) auxiliary memory for the traversal state itself, independent of tree depth [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On an integrated GPU, ESVO (a variant) achieves 95.0 Mrays/s for primary rays on the Sponza 2k scene (499k * 8³ voxels) [S7, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Eliminates recursion limits; greedy approach reduces unnecessary node visits; fixed-size leaves optimize cache usage.
-*   **Limitations:** False positives require backtracking, which can be costly; fixed leaf size may waste memory in sparse regions; performance degrades with high branch divergence on GPUs if not optimized [S2, S7, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When targeting hardware without recursion support (GPUs, HLSL) or when stack depth is unpredictable.
-*   **When not to use:** When memory bandwidth is the primary bottleneck and node size is large, or when the scene is extremely dense with no empty space to skip.
+*   **Strengths:** Eliminates recursion limits; greedy approach reduces unnecessary node visits; fixed-size leaves optimize cache usage [S2, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** False positives require backtracking, which can be costly; fixed leaf size may waste memory in sparse regions [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When targeting hardware without recursion support (GPUs, HLSL) or when stack depth is unpredictable [S2, S4, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When memory bandwidth is the primary bottleneck and node size is large, or when the scene is extremely dense with no empty space to skip [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Iterative Octree Traversal originated as a solution to the recursion limitations in HLSL (High-Level Shading Language) and GPU architectures, which do not support general method recursion [S2, S4, unofficial]. It was developed to enable real-time raycasting of sparse voxel octrees on GPUs by simulating the call stack explicitly [S2, unofficial]. The method is closely related to the ESVO (Efficient Sparse Voxel Octrees) algorithm, which popularized the use of small, localized node structures for fast traversal [S7, unofficial].
